<div style="background-color: #1a5276; padding: 20px; text-align: center; color: white; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Visiting Our Country – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Visiting Our Country</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> Interesting Things to See</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Analyze the tone and implied meaning in discussions. Interactive: Respond appropriately when participating in discussions on familiar topics.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can recognize tone and implied meaning in conversations about visitor safety by identifying key concerns and suggested precautions (e.g., "They said Panama is generally safe, but emphasized being cautious in crowded areas, which suggests concern about pickpocketing.").<br>Can identify key attractions from a conversation about a city tour (e.g., "They said the Panama Canal, the Contemporary Art Museum, and the local beaches are good places to see!").<br>Can interpret implied suggestions and respond appropriately when discussing travel plans (e.g., "The guide suggested taking a bus because it makes it easy to get around in the city.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 34%; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher begins by playing short, distinct audio clips of sounds associated with popular Panamanian locations (e.g., ship passing through the Canal, street music from Casco Viejo, waves on a beach, a bustling market). Students listen and guess the location or type of place. The teacher then shows images of these locations and asks students to brainstorm adjectives to describe them (e.g., "breathtaking," "tranquil," "interesting," "unique"). Introduce key vocabulary: *attraction*, *landmark*, *market*, *beach*, *museum*, *history*, *explore*, *recommend*, *visit*, *safe*.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of the new vocabulary words, paying close attention to contrastive sounds like /st/ in "stay" and /ks/ in "explain," and reduced syllables in "interesting" (/ˈɪn.trə.stɪŋ/) and "restaurant" (/ˈres.trɒnt/). For example, the teacher says "Panama has many *interesting* attractions to *explore*." Students repeat chorally and individually. The teacher points to images while saying words like "This is a must-see *landmark*," connecting words to visual meaning.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the introduced vocabulary and concepts.<br>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If something is a 'must-see attraction,' does it mean you should definitely go, or you don't have to go?" (Expected answer: "Definitely go!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a place is 'tranquil,' is it noisy and busy, or calm and peaceful?" (Expected answer: "Calm and peaceful!")</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Visiting Our Country")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An authentic audio recording of a dialogue between a local Panamanian and a tourist discussing a city tour and safety tips. The dialogue is conversational, B1.1 level, and incorporates target grammar (past simple/present perfect, future *will/going to*, reported speech) and vocabulary (e.g., *location*, *guide*, *itinerary*, *precaution*, *safety*, *transportation*).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Receptive: Analyze the tone and implied meaning in discussions. Interactive: Respond appropriately when participating in discussions on familiar topics.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b><br>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Listening:</b> Listen to the dialogue once. What are the two speakers mainly discussing? (A. Local food; B. Travel plans and safety in Panama; C. Panamanian history).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Specific Information:</b> Listen a second time.
                        <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identify three specific attractions mentioned for the city tour. (e.g., Panama Canal, Casco Viejo, seafood market).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What implied suggestion did the local make about being in crowded areas? How can you tell their tone? (e.g., "They emphasized being cautious, suggesting concern about pickpocketing.").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The tourist mentioned, "I heard that the best seafood is found in the seafood market." Is this an example of reported speech?</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Interpreting Tone and Implied Meaning:</b> Listen again to specific segments. For example, "The local said, 'It's generally safe, but you *really* need to watch your belongings in crowded places.' What does the word 'really' imply about the local's concern?"</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>(Accuracy focus on Listening)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening for specific information (attractions, safety tips, directions), identifying grammatical structures (past simple/present perfect contrast, future will/going to, reported speech), and using target vocabulary (e.g., *area*, *location*, *plan*, *direction*, *accommodations*, *guide*, *explain*, *advise*).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b><br>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: Listen and Complete:</b> Students receive a worksheet with cloze sentences derived from the audio dialogue. They listen to specific excerpts again and fill in the blanks with target vocabulary (e.g., "Casco Viejo _______ (was) once a neglected ____ (area), but it ____ ____ (has become) a popular destination.") or identify if a sentence uses 'will' or 'going to' (e.g., "I ____ (will) book a tour for us tomorrow." "We're ____ __ (going to) try that new restaurant."). Focus on pronunciation of words like "interesting" and "restaurant."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Substitution Drill – Reported Speech:</b> The teacher provides sentences from the dialogue (e.g., "The local said, 'You should *visit* the Canal.'"). Students listen and then practice rephrasing them using reported speech, individually and in pairs (e.g., "They said that we *should visit* the Canal."). The teacher provides new prompts with verbs like "recommend," "advise," "explain" to practice different reporting verbs.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction – Following Directions:</b> Students work in pairs. Each student receives a simple map of a fictional town in Panama with several landmarks (e.g., *museum*, *market*, *beach*, *cathedral*). One student describes how to get from point A to point B using prepositions (*go straight*, *turn right*, *turn left*, *across from*, *next to*, *between*, *behind*, *opposite*, *in front of*), while the other listens and traces the route on their map. They then switch roles. For example, "From the *cathedral*, *go straight* two *blocks*, then *turn right*. The *museum* is *across from* the park."</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Visiting Our Country")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Role-Play: "Planning a Panama City Adventure". Students work in groups of three. One student acts as a Panamanian *guide*, and the other two are *tourists* visiting Panama. The tourists have a few ideas of things they want to see and ask for recommendations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b><br>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The *tourists* initiate by asking for recommendations on local *attractions* (e.g., "What places should my friend *visit*?" "We're planning to *explore* the city; what do you *recommend*?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The *guide* responds by describing popular *landmarks* (e.g., "The *museum showcases* our cultural heritage. The *Panama Canal* is a *must-see*.") and offering suggestions for an *itinerary* using future tenses (e.g., "I *will* book a tour for you, and we're *going to* try the seafood *market*."). They also give directions clearly, ensuring everyone understands (Sociolinguistic competence).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The *guide* also *advises* on *safety precautions* (e.g., "They said that Panama is generally *safe*, but emphasized being cautious in crowded *areas*, which suggests concern about pickpocketing."). The *tourists* must *listen* to the implied meaning and respond appropriately (e.g., "Thanks for the *precaution*! We *will* keep that in mind.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students should actively *listen* to each other, *interpret* implied suggestions, and ask clarifying questions if needed (e.g., "You mentioned the *transportation* is easy. Did you mean buses or taxis?").</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the role-play and a short peer assessment after.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform the "Planning a Panama City Adventure" role-play in their groups.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b><br>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible Pronunciation:</b> Clear pronunciation of target vocabulary and sounds (e.g., /st/, /ks/, reduced syllables).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Appropriately uses at least 5-7 new vocabulary words (e.g., *attraction*, *landmark*, *precaution*, *guide*, *explore*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Fluency:</b> Communicates ideas clearly and with reasonable flow, responding appropriately to implied meanings.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration and Interaction:</b> Actively listens and responds to group members, participates in group planning, offering directions and suggestions clearly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar Application:</b> Uses past simple/present perfect and future (will/going to) structures correctly when contrasting actions or expressing intentions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Reported Speech:</b> Attempts to use reported speech when referencing what others said (e.g., "They said that...").</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts:<br>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One interesting thing I heard about Panama today was..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"A new word I learned and can use to describe a place is..."</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b><br>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging about listening for implied meaning or tone today, and how did you try to overcome it?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do you feel now about asking for and giving recommendations or directions in English?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b><br>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this listening lesson? Were students able to analyze tone and implied meaning effectively?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson (Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension) to ensure continued vocabulary acquisition and deeper contextual understanding? For example, should I provide a glossary or a guided reading strategy? Did the pronunciation practice help with listening comprehension?</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 2, students will read a descriptive text about Panamanian attractions, reinforcing the vocabulary introduced today and understanding the context in written form, preparing them for more detailed information extraction.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>